WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.729
Once the decision is framed, the next question is what the decision is trying to achieve.

2
00:00:05.729 --> 00:00:09.368
Objectives are the outcomes or conditions that matter.

3
00:00:09.368 --> 00:00:12.156
Values explain why those outcomes matter.

4
00:00:12.156 --> 00:00:17.575
Criteria turn those objectives into standards that can be used to compare options.

5
00:00:17.575 --> 00:00:24.621
Without that structure, teams may appear to agree while each person is optimizing for something different.

6
00:00:24.621 --> 00:00:36.235
For example, one person may want the fastest solution, another may want the lowest cost, another may want the safest implementation, and another may want the most learning value.

7
00:00:36.235 --> 00:00:39.487
None of those priorities is automatically wrong.

8
00:00:39.487 --> 00:00:42.893
The problem is that they produce different choices.

9
00:00:42.893 --> 00:00:53.578
Decision science makes the priorities visible so the tradeoffs can be discussed honestly instead of hidden inside vague words like best, strategic, or efficient.

10
00:00:53.578 --> 00:00:57.062
It helps to separate must-haves from nice-to-haves.

11
00:00:57.062 --> 00:01:07.437
A must-have is a condition an option must satisfy to remain viable, such as a legal requirement, a critical safety requirement, or a compatibility constraint.

12
00:01:07.437 --> 00:01:12.392
A nice-to-have improves the option but does not define viability by itself.

13
00:01:12.392 --> 00:01:14.792
Confusing the two creates friction.

14
00:01:14.792 --> 00:01:21.373
Teams either reject workable options too early or accept options that fail important requirements.

15
00:01:21.373 --> 00:01:32.290
Common criteria include quality, cost, speed, safety, resilience, ethics, reversibility, maintainability, user impact, operational burden, and learning value.

16
00:01:32.290 --> 00:01:41.891
In technical and security work, criteria might include blast radius, detection coverage, recovery time, auditability, and ability to roll back.

17
00:01:41.891 --> 00:01:49.633
In learning design, criteria might include clarity, accessibility, engagement, transfer, and assessment quality.

18
00:01:49.633 --> 00:01:52.576
Unclear objectives create false agreement.

19
00:01:52.576 --> 00:01:55.673
Everyone nods until the real tradeoff appears.

20
00:01:55.673 --> 00:02:01.015
Clear objectives do not remove disagreement, but they make disagreement useful.

21
00:02:01.015 --> 00:02:12.164
They let the group ask which objective should dominate in this case, which tradeoffs are acceptable, and which option best fits the values and constraints of the decision.
